to other SVG traces on the same subplot. SVG traces with higher
`zorder` appear in front of those with lower `zorder`. Integer.}

\item{row}{Subplot grid row (figures created with
\code{\link{make_subplots}()} only). May be \code{"all"} or a vector
to address several cells.}

\item{col}{Subplot grid column (figures created with
\code{\link{make_subplots}()} only). May be \code{"all"} or a vector
to address several cells.}

\item{secondary_y}{If \code{TRUE}, use the secondary y axis of the
subplot.}

\item{skip_invalid}{If \code{TRUE}, silently drop invalid properties
instead of raising an error.}

\item{arg}{Optional named list, or graph object of the same type, to
initialise properties from (constructors). Named arguments take precedence.}
}
\value{
\code{go_*()} functions return a graph object;
  \code{add_*()} functions return the modified figure.
}
\description{
The scatter trace type encompasses line charts, scatter charts, text
charts, and bubble charts. The data visualized as scatter point or lines
is set in `x` and `y`. Text (appearing either on the chart or on hover
only) is via `text`. Bubble charts are achieved by setting `marker.size`
and/or `marker.color` to numerical arrays.
}
\details{
\code{go_scatter()} creates a validated graph object (a named list).
\code{add_scatter()} creates one and adds it to a figure.


Nested objects are given as named lists or with their constructors, which
are linked from the arguments below. Every property is checked against
the plotly.js schema when the object is created or modified (see
\code{\link{plotly_obj-modify}}). One-sided formulas (\code{~col}) are
evaluated in the data, and then checked, when the trace is added with
\code{add_<trace>()} or \code{add_trace()}.
}
\examples{
go_scatter(x = 1:3, y = c(2, 1, 3), mode = "markers", marker = go_scatter_marker(color = "red"))
}
\seealso{
\code{\link{go_figure}()}, \code{\link{add_trace}()}
}
\concept{traces}
